Institutional identity
Full name:     洛陽大伯公宮 (Luòyáng Dà Bó Gōng Gōng)
               Loyang Tua Pek Kong Temple (Hokkien romanization)

Components:
  洛陽  (Luòyáng)  — Loyang (geographic locality name)
  大伯公 (Dà Bó Gōng / Tua Pek Kong) — principal deity:
                    "Great Senior Uncle" — the deified
                    Chinese god of prosperity, fortune,
                    and protection (Fude Zhenshen)
  宮    (Gōng)     — Palace/Temple (high-status temple
                    designation, distinct from 寺 monastery,
                    廟 folk temple, or 堂 hall)

Naming convention: DEITY-NAMED + LOCALITY
  The name encodes both the principal deity and
  the geographic location. The 宮 designation
  signals higher institutional prestige than 廟.

Institutional category: MULTI-FAITH TEMPLE
  Explicitly integrating Taoism, Buddhism, Hinduism,
  and the Malayan Islamic Datuk Kong tradition within
  a single compound — the most institutionally complex
  site documented in this corpus.
Founding narrative: A group of fishing buddies spotted statues of Buddhist, Hindu, and Taoist deities strewn across a beach in the Loyang industrial area in the 1980s. They built a modest hut of bricks and zinc sheets to house the abandoned statues. A gentleman then claimed he received a sign from Allah directing them to include a keramat for Datuk Kong, and after multiple people made the same claim, a place was set aside for the Muslim keramat in honour of Datuk Kong. 
The miraculous founding proof: In 1996, the temple was burnt to the ground by a fire. The majority of the statues were affected except the statue of Tua Pek Kong, the Taoist god of prosperity, which was the only undamaged statue. The temple was subsequently named after Tua Pek Kong, the only unscathed statue after the fire.
This establishes a founding narrative of miraculous fire-proof survival — the same category as Nanhai Guanyin Temple's 南海飛來 ("Flew from the South Sea") inscription. The deity's identity and the institution's name are both validated by a narrative of supernatural authentication. The surviving statue itself becomes a relic whose survival legitimates the entire institution. It also establishes a principle: Tua Pek Kong was not the original intended principal deity (the temple began as a multi-deity shelter); his primacy emerged through the fire's discriminating destruction.
