The compound's architectural differentiation strategy:
The temple has been divided into sections for Taoism, Buddhism, Hinduism, and Datuk Kong respectively. One can notice some form of distinct boundaries when looking at the different architecture styles from the outside; however, on the inside, there is no clear physical boundaries between the sections to allow devotees to cross freely between one another.
This is the critical architectural-theological decision of the compound. Each tradition receives distinct exterior architectural markers (Xieshan roof → Taoist, gopuram → Hindu, onion dome → Datuk Kong) that signal separate traditions to approaching visitors. But the interior eliminates physical barriers — devotees can move freely from one tradition's space to another. The compound simultaneously differentiates and integrates: it respects each tradition's architectural identity while refusing to enclose any tradition in its own separate space.
