Identifying the figures
The Tai Sui (太歲, Tài Suì)
The Tai Sui are a set of 60 divine generals, each
governing one year of the 60-year sexagenary cycle
(the combination of the Ten Heavenly Stems and
Twelve Earthly Branches). Each year falls under
the protection and administration of one specific
Tai Sui general.

Function:   cosmological and calendrical administration
            -- governing fortune, fate, and the progression
               of time for devotees born in a given year
Register:   Taoist / folk-religious
Visual:     typically depicted as 60 seated generals
            in formal robes and court headdresses,
            arranged in rows by year

Devotional practice:
  Devotees identify the Tai Sui corresponding to their
  birth year and pray to that specific general for
  protection and blessings. Particularly important at
  the start of the Chinese New Year when the annual
  Tai Sui changes.
The Bodhisattva above
A single Guanyin (觀世音菩薩) figure -- whose compassionate intercession is invoked 
broadly across cosmological contexts  is positioned above
and behind the Tai Sui array — elevated, larger than
the Tai Sui figures below, surmounting the entire
configuration.
